<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{ meta.lang }" class="notranslate">

{@partial partials/head.sort}

<body class="page-home">

  {@partial partials/nav.sort}

  <main id="main-content">

    <!-- Hero -->
    <header class="hero" id="overview">
      <div class="shell hero-inner">
        <div>
          <h1>Frontmatter,<br><em>specified.</em></h1>
          <p class="lede">Lima is a small data format for frontmatter in which every line has one meaning &mdash; written down in a complete specification, the same in TypeScript, Rust, and Go, and with none of YAML&rsquo;s unused machinery.</p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="btn-primary" href="/docs/guide/">Get started</a>
            <a class="btn-link" href="/packages/">Installation</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="sample">
          <figcaption class="sample-head"><span class="sample-file"><img class="sample-ico" src="/favicon.svg" alt="" width="14" height="14" /> ARTICLE.LIMA</span><span>CORE 1.0</span></figcaption>
          <pre><code><span class="line"><span class="key">title</span><span class="punct">:</span> <span class="str">A smaller format</span></span><span class="line"><span class="key">published</span><span class="punct">:</span> 2026-09-08</span><span class="line"><span class="key">authors</span><span class="punct">:</span></span><span class="line">  <span class="punct">-</span> <span class="key">name</span><span class="punct">:</span> <span class="str">Michael</span></span><span class="line">    <span class="key">role</span><span class="punct">:</span> <span class="str">author</span></span><span class="line"><span class="key">tags</span><span class="punct">:</span> <span class="sample-tags"><span class="punct">[</span>frontmatter<span class="punct">,</span> specification<span class="punct">]</span></span></span></code></pre>
          <div class="sample-result">PARSED &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; <b>4 keys &middot; 0 diagnostics</b></div>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Status strip -->
    <aside class="status">
      <div class="shell status-inner">
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-label">Conformance status</span><span class="stat-value">ALL IMPLEMENTATIONS PASS</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-label">Core { site.data.lima.language.core_version }</span><span class="stat-value">{ site.data.lima.corpus.default.core_cases | number('en-US') } cases</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-label">Differential test</span><span class="stat-value">{ site.data.lima.differential_test.parses_per_implementation | number('en-US') } parses / run</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-label">Supply chain</span><span class="stat-value">{ site.data.lima.packages.runtime_dependencies } dependencies</span></div>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <!-- Guarantees -->
    <section class="section" id="why" aria-labelledby="why-h">
      <div class="shell layout">
        <p class="kicker">01 / Guarantees</p>
        <div>
          <h2 id="why-h">Every line.<br>One meaning.</h2>
          <p class="intro">Fifteen lines of frontmatter deserve the same answer everywhere. Lima writes that answer down &mdash; grammar, types, limits, and errors &mdash; and three independent implementations show that they give it.</p>
          <div class="reasons">
            <article class="reason">
              <span class="reason-no">01</span>
              <h3>Nothing lost without a word</h3>
              <p>In strict mode every line belongs to a key, a block, or a scalar, or it is an error at its line &mdash; with a hint where a YAML habit such as <code>description: &gt;</code> is the cause.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="reason">
              <span class="reason-no">02</span>
              <h3>One answer in every language</h3>
              <p>TypeScript, Rust, and Go pass the same { site.data.lima.corpus.default.core_cases | number('en-US') } Core and { site.data.lima.corpus.default.references_cases | number('en-US') } References cases, error codes and lines included, and agree on { site.data.lima.differential_test.documents | number('en-US') } random documents in every CI run.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="reason">
              <span class="reason-no">03</span>
              <h3>Types without guessing</h3>
              <p><code>true</code> and <code>false</code> are the only booleans, so <code>country: NO</code> stays <code>"NO"</code>. Fixed date formats, safe integers, hard limits &mdash; one behaviour, no schema to choose.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why not YAML / TOML / JSON -->
    <section class="section" id="why-not" aria-labelledby="whynot-h">
      <div class="shell layout">
        <p class="kicker">02 / Why not</p>
        <div>
          <h2 id="whynot-h">YAML, TOML or JSON?</h2>
          <p class="intro">Frontmatter is a few lines of key&ndash;value data. Each obvious choice carries a cost Lima was built to avoid.</p>
          <div class="reasons">
            <article class="reason">
              <span class="reason-no">YAML</span>
              <h3>Too large a grammar</h3>
              <p>Frontmatter uses a small fraction of it. The unused parts are exactly where implementations diverge from each other and where the past CVEs sit.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="reason">
              <span class="reason-no">TOML</span>
              <h3>Verbose once nested</h3>
              <p>Its <code>[section]</code> / <code>key = value</code> form is noisier than indentation for nested metadata, and it has no equivalent of References.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="reason">
              <span class="reason-no">JSON</span>
              <h3>Awkward to hand-author</h3>
              <p>No comments, every key and string quoted, no multi-line strings &mdash; workable for machines, tedious to write and diff as frontmatter.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Security scope -->
    <section class="section" id="security" aria-labelledby="sec-h">
      <div class="shell layout">
        <p class="kicker">03 / Security scope</p>
        <div>
          <h2 id="sec-h">A narrower<br>attack surface.</h2>
          <p class="intro">No tags, anchors, aliases, merge keys, directives, or multi-document streams: the machinery that is not there cannot misbehave. Not a claim that Lima is unhackable &mdash; a narrower one: two well-known YAML attack classes have no construct to target, because Lima never implemented the construct. This is worth something only where it applies &mdash; when something in your pipeline parses frontmatter you don&rsquo;t fully control (a CMS, user submissions, a multi-tenant platform), not for your own files.</p>
          <div class="security">
            <article>
              <code>Executable tags</code>
              <h3>No tag system</h3>
              <p>PyYAML&rsquo;s <code>!!python/object/new</code> tag allowed arbitrary code execution (CVE-2020-1747). Lima has no tags, custom or built-in &mdash; a scalar is a string, number, boolean, null, or date, and nothing else.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <code>Alias expansion</code>
              <h3>No anchors or aliases</h3>
              <p>Repeated alias expansion let a few kilobytes of YAML allocate gigabytes (CVE-2019-11253, the Kubernetes API server&rsquo;s &ldquo;Billion Laughs&rdquo;). Lima has no alias expansion; References 2.0 resolves by bounded structural copy, capped at 65,536 result nodes &mdash; a few kilobytes cannot balloon without limit.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Measurements -->
    <section class="section" id="measure" aria-labelledby="measure-h">
      <div class="shell layout">
        <p class="kicker">04 / Measurements</p>
        <div>
          <h2 id="measure-h">Small as<br>a consequence.</h2>
          <p class="intro">Lima is smaller because it does less &mdash; the evidence, not the goal. The comparison that does the frontmatter job on both sides, Lima Core { site.data.lima.language.core_version } against js-yaml&rsquo;s <code>load()</code> path: less code, and js-yaml&rsquo;s download is { site.data.lima.package_sizes.js_yaml_ratio_to_lima | number('en-US') } times Lima&rsquo;s.</p>
          <div class="ledger">
            <div class="ledger-head"><span>&nbsp;</span><span>Code</span><span>Package</span></div>
            <div class="ledger-row"><strong>Lima Core { site.data.lima.language.core_version }</strong><span class="measure">{ site.data.lima.word_counts.implementations.lima_core_code | number('en-US') }<small>words</small></span><span class="measure">{ site.data.lima.package_sizes.lima_npm_tarball_kilobytes_decimal | number('en-US') }<small>KB&nbsp;&dagger;</small></span></div>
            <div class="ledger-row"><strong>js-yaml { site.data.lima.word_counts.implementations.js_yaml_version } <code>load()</code></strong><span class="measure">{ site.data.lima.word_counts.implementations.js_yaml_code | number('en-US') }<small>words</small></span><span class="measure">{ site.data.lima.package_sizes.js_yaml_npm_tarball_kilobytes_decimal | number('en-US') }<small>KB</small></span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="ledger-note">&dagger; The <code>@limaformat/lima</code> { site.data.lima.packages.version } package also bundles References { site.data.lima.language.references_version } &mdash; an optional layer ({ site.data.lima.word_counts.implementations.lima_references_code | number('en-US') } more words) with no YAML counterpart &mdash; so the download comparison is conservative. js-yaml&rsquo;s path additionally carries anchors and aliases, merge keys, multi-document streams, directives, and { site.data.lima.word_counts.implementations.js_yaml_tag_implementations | number('en-US') } tag implementations that ordinary frontmatter never needs &mdash; the same constructs behind the two CVEs below. Lima Core&rsquo;s { site.data.lima.word_counts.implementations.lima_core_code | number('en-US') } words also carry { site.data.lima.word_counts.implementations.lima_core_comments | number('en-US') } words of comments, many tying an implementation line to a spec section. The full accounting &mdash; including the Lima Core { site.data.lima.language.core_version } spec at { site.data.lima.word_counts.specifications.lima_core | number('en-US') } words against YAML 1.2.2&rsquo;s { site.data.lima.word_counts.specifications.yaml_1_2_2 | number('en-US') } &mdash; is in the <a href="https://github.com/limaformat/lima#how-much-smaller-actually">README</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Packages -->
    <section class="section" id="packages" aria-labelledby="pkg-h">
      <div class="shell layout">
        <p class="kicker">05 / Packages</p>
        <div>
          <h2 id="pkg-h">Three implementations.<br>One contract.</h2>
          <p class="intro">Choose the package native to your stack. The supported syntax and conformance fixtures remain the same.</p>
          <div class="impl-grid">
            {#each site.packages as pkg}
            <a class="impl" href="{ pkg.url }">
              <span class="impl-name">{ pkg.lang }</span>
              <code class="impl-cmd">{ pkg.install }</code>
              <span class="impl-reg">{ pkg.badge } &middot; v{ site.data.lima.packages.version } &rarr;</span>
            </a>
            {/each}
          </div>
          <p class="ledger-note">Editor support: a <a href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=limaformat.vscode-lima" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VS Code extension</a> ships today; <a href="/editors/">more editors</a> run on a language server.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  {@partial partials/footer.sort}

</body>

</html>
